file 1 has `c 1\\nc 2\\na 3\\n` and we're matching \"c\": skipMatch will advance past both \"c\" entries and land on \"a 3\", setting prevKey1 to \"c\", then the main loop detects \"a\" < \"c\" and catches the unsorted violation. Now I'm checking what happens when skipMatch encounters multiple matching keys in a row... at \"c 1\" → skip, advance to \"c 2\". Skip, advance to \"a 3\". Key \"a\" != \"c\" → break.\n- After skip: lr1 at \"a 3\".\n- Main loop: key1 = \"a\", prevKey1 = \"c\". \"a\" < \"c\" → caught.\n\nOK, all good.\n\nNow, the important remaining question: for the `joinMatch` checkOrder path, do I also need to handle the `skipMatch` case? I.e., when `suppressPaired` is true, should I also collect groups and check before skipping?\n\nActually, in `skipMatch`, we don't OUTPUT anything. So even if we skip through unsorted lines, no incorrect output is produced. The unsorted detection happens at the next check point (main loop top or post-loop drain).\n\nLet me trace through a concrete example to see if GNU join detects unsorted lines within skipMatch earlier: File1 has \"a 1\", \"c 3\", \"b 2\" and File2 has \"a X\", \"b Y\", \"c Z\" with `--check-order -v 1`. After matching \"a\", both files skip their \"a\" lines, leaving lr1 at \"c 3\" and lr2 at \"b Y\". In the main loop, \"c\" > \"b\" so lr2 advances to \"c Z\", then both match on \"c\" and enter skipMatch again, but File1's \"c\" lines are exhausted while File2 still has more to process.\n\nThe error is caught in the post-loop drain when lr1 has \"b 2\" remaining but lr2 is at EOF—comparing \"b\" against the previous key \"c\" reveals the sort violation.\n\nSince no output is produced and the error is detected, skipMatch doesn't need modification. Now I'll start implementing the changes, being careful about the order of edits across files. I need to carefully plan these edits to main.go, making sure each replacement string is unique so the edits don't interfere with each other. I'll start by modifying 